        "Vertebral, Anal, Cardiac, Tracheoesophageal, Renal, Limb anomalies. ≥ 3 defects = VACTERL. Surgical correction sequenced.",
        "## VACTERL acronym\n\n- **V**ertebral anomalies (60-80 %): hemivertebrae, butterfly vertebrae, scoliosis\n- **A**nal atresia / imperforate anus (55-90 %)\n- **C**ardiac defects (40-80 %): VSD, ASD, TOF, PDA\n- **T**racheoesophageal fistula / esophageal atresia (50-80 %)\n- **R**enal anomalies (50-80 %): agenesis, dysplasia, vesicoureteral reflux\n- **L**imb defects (40-50 %): radial dysplasia, polydactyly\n\n### Diagnosis\n- ≥ 3 component defects\n- Exclude single-gene syndromes (Fanconi, CHARGE)\n\n## Workup at diagnosis\n\n### Imaging / studies\n- **CXR** + abdominal X-ray (bowel gas, vertebrae)\n- **Echocardiogram** (cardiac)\n- **Renal US** (anomalies)\n- **Spine US** или MRI (tethered cord exclusion)\n- **Esophagogram** или endoscopy (TEF/EA suspicion)\n- **Abdominal US** (anorectal malformation)\n- **Limb radiographs** (radial defect)\n\n### Genetics\n- **Karyotype + microarray (CMA)**\n- **Fanconi anemia testing** (chromosomal breakage study) — important rule-out\n- VACTERL is sporadic — no specific gene yet identified\n\n## Component management\n\n### Esophageal atresia / TEF\n- **Type C** (proximal pouch + distal TEF) — 85 % cases\n- **Surgical repair** typical first 24-72 h life\n- **NG-tube / Replogle** suction proximal pouch (prevent aspiration)\n- **NPO** + IV fluids until repair\n- **Anastomotic leak** 10-20 %\n- **Gap > 2 cm** — staged repair (cervical esophagostomy + gastric pull-up later)\n\n### Imperforate anus\n- **Low** (subcutaneous fistula) — perineal anoplasty\n- **High** (cloacal type) — colostomy first → posterior sagittal anorectoplasty (PSARP) 6-12 мес → colostomy closure later\n- **Long-term**: continence issues, constipation\n\n### Cardiac\n- See art-cardiac-surgery (general approach)\n- Surgery sequence based on cardiac severity vs other defects\n\n### Vertebral / spinal\n- Tethered cord 5-15 % — surgery if symptomatic\n- Scoliosis development monitored\n\n### Renal\n- Hydronephrosis treatment\n- VUR — antibiotic prophylaxis or surgical reflux correction\n- Solitary kidney — long-term nephrology follow-up\n\n### Limb\n- Radial dysplasia — orthopedics/hand surgery (centralization, pollicization)\n\n## Coordinated approach\n\nVACTERL infants need MULTIDISCIPLINARY team:\n- Neonatology\n- Cardiac surgery / cardiology\n- Pediatric surgery (TEF + anus + abdomen)\n- Orthopedics (limb + spine)\n- Urology (renal)\n- Genetics + counseling\n- Speech therapy (later — voice / swallow post-TEF)\n- Long-term follow-up clinic\n\n## Outcomes\n\n- Mortality 25-30 % first year (severe cardiac + multi-system)\n- Survivors often achieve good function-level long-term\n- Continence + cardiac + spine — main long-term challenges\n\n## Calculator Bordik\n- neo-cchd-pulse-oximetry\n- neo-discharge-criteria\n\n## Источники\n- Solomon BD. J Pediatr Genet 2018;7:104\n- Brosens E et al. Birth Defects Res Part C 2014;102:316\n- AAP Section on Surgery 2024",
